Endometrial Cancer
Haematology & Oncology
Postmenopausal vaginal bleeding, Heavy irregular perimenopausal bleeding, History of unopposed oestrogen/obesity, Thickened endometrium/malignant cells on biopsy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-017
Reason for Visit: Sudden, heavy postmenopausal vaginal bleeding with clots and deep pelvic cramping requiring urgent assessment for haemodynamic stability and acute blood loss.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 62-year-old postmenopausal female experiencing an abrupt, heavy vaginal haemorrhage soaking a large pad every 30 minutes. Urgent physical evaluation was required to assess the rate of haemorrhage and prevent profound hypovolaemia, as leaving this unassessed until the morning posed an unacceptable risk of cardiovascular compromise.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I haven't had a period in ten years, but tonight I suddenly started gushing blood with large clots, and my lower stomach is cramping terribly."
History of Present Illness: The patient is 12 years postmenopausal. They report noticing minor, pink-tinged spotting on toilet paper intermittently over the past 3 weeks, which they dismissed. Tonight, they experienced an abrupt onset of heavy, bright red vaginal bleeding accompanied by deep pelvic pain and severe cramping lower abdominal discomfort. They report profound generalized fatigue over the last month but deny purulent or foul-smelling discharge. The patient has a history of severe obesity and Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.
Functional Status: Currently resting on the couch, frightened to stand due to the sensation of gushing blood.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; significant fall risk if orthostatic hypotension develops.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 108 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 112 Kg
General: Anxious, pale adult. Alert and oriented, but clearly distressed by the sudden bleeding.
Physical Exam:
Abdomen: Morbidly obese. Deep palpation elicits moderate suprapubic tenderness and cramping. No rigid peritonitis or rebound tenderness. No palpable abdominal masses.
Pelvic (Visual Inspection): Active, heavy bright red bleeding with large dark clots visible at the vaginal introitus. Bimanual examination deferred due to heavy bleeding and to avoid inducing further trauma prior to specialist imaging.
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm. Capillary refill 2 seconds. No postural drop tested due to active bleeding.
Environmental Observations: Heavily soaked towels utilized on the couch.
Medication Reconciliation: Metformin and Ramipril confirmed. No hormone replacement therapy or anticoagulants currently prescribed.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Endometrial Cancer - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Haemorrhage)
Differentials:

Atrophic Vaginitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the sheer volume of haemorrhage with large clots and severe cramping, which is completely out of proportion to simple atrophic spotting.

Cervical Cancer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the lack of foul-smelling discharge, negative history for postcoital bleeding, and the classic postmenopausal presentation with severe obesity pointing strongly toward an endometrial source.

Endometrial Polyps - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the heavy, sudden volume in a postmenopausal woman with high-risk metabolic factors, though a biopsy is ultimately required to distinguish from malignancy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Any heavy postmenopausal bleeding is an absolute red flag for endometrial malignancy until proven otherwise. This patient's presentation of abrupt, heavy haemorrhage with clots and cramping, combined with the high-risk metabolic profile (obesity, diabetes), strongly suggests advanced endometrial cancer. The tachycardia indicates early haemodynamic compensation for acute blood loss. Delaying assessment until normal clinic hours would expose the patient to progressive, unmonitored hypovolaemia, potential syncope, and shock. The urgent after-hours visit was essential to visually quantify the haemorrhage, establish clinical stability, and arrange immediate emergency gynaecological review.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department for acute haemostasis, haemodynamic monitoring, and an urgent transvaginal pelvic ultrasound (anticipating a thickened endometrial echo complex >4 mm) leading to formal biopsy.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Remain seated or lying down while awaiting transport. By avoiding upright postures, you prevent sudden drops in blood pressure to your brain, drastically reducing your risk of fainting.

Use thick maternity pads instead of tampons. By allowing the blood to exit freely and strictly avoiding internal insertion, you prevent further mechanical trauma to the cervix or uterus.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department is required. Call 000 if you suddenly feel extremely dizzy, short of breath, or if the bleeding accelerates to soaking through a pad in less than 15 minutes.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Postmenopausal bleeding must always be investigated aggressively at the hospital to determine the exact source; conditions can change rapidly.
Clinical Course: The bleeding is significant and causing your heart to race. The hospital will use specific scans and potentially minor procedures to stop the bleeding and take a tissue sample.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not drive yourself to the hospital. Have a relative or ambulance transport you. Keep your legs elevated if you feel lightheaded.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Bleeding after menopause is never normal, and given your history, we must suspect a growth in the lining of the womb. Urgent hospital care is needed to stop the blood loss safely and arrange a biopsy.
Actionable Steps: Rest quietly. Do not use tampons. Prepare for immediate transfer to the emergency department.

